---
import '../../styles/development-editorial.css';
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import LegacyHeader from "../../components/LegacyHeader.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../../components/Hero.astro";
import ButtonPrimary from "../../components/ButtonPrimary.astro";
import ButtonSecondary from "../../components/ButtonSecondary.astro";
import DevelopmentStackJobs from "../../components/DevelopmentStackJobs.astro";
const canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/development/sveltekit/";
const proof=[
  {
    "title": "Prerender what is stable",
    "text": "Content pages can be built ahead of time. Routes with current or private data run on the server, and both return real HTML."
  },
  {
    "title": "Forms tested both ways",
    "text": "We test a normal submission with JavaScript disabled, then the enhanced interaction with it enabled."
  },
  {
    "title": "Adapter matched to the host",
    "text": "We check that the proposed host runs the required adapter before choosing it, not after the first failed deploy."
  }
];
const checks=[
  {
    "title": "Crawlable pages",
    "text": "Search engines need clean URLs, internal links, server-rendered content where needed and sensible indexation controls."
  },
  {
    "title": "Content structure",
    "text": "Pages need headings, body copy, proof, FAQs and conversion points that support SEO rather than sit behind visual components."
  },
  {
    "title": "Performance and Core Web Vitals",
    "text": "We reduce bloat, unnecessary scripts, layout shifts and render delays that weaken user experience."
  },
  {
    "title": "Redirect and migration safety",
    "text": "When replacing an old site, URL mapping and launch QA matter more than a prettier homepage."
  },
  {
    "title": "Schema and metadata",
    "text": "Titles, descriptions, canonical tags, structured data and Open Graph basics should be built in, not bolted on later."
  },
  {
    "title": "Analytics and forms",
    "text": "A site that cannot track enquiries properly makes SEO reporting weaker than it needs to be."
  }
];
const process=[
  {
    "title": "Audit",
    "text": "Review the current site, rankings, crawl behaviour, content, analytics and conversion risks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Plan",
    "text": "Map templates, URLs, content sections, redirects, schema and development priorities."
  },
  {
    "title": "Build",
    "text": "Develop the site around speed, accessibility, crawlability and editorial control."
  },
  {
    "title": "QA",
    "text": "Test pages, metadata, forms, mobile layouts, redirects, schema and indexation settings."
  },
  {
    "title": "Launch",
    "text": "Deploy with a launch checklist, Search Console monitoring and post-launch crawl checks."
  },
  {
    "title": "Improve",
    "text": "Use ranking, crawl and conversion data to refine pages after launch."
  }
];
const faqs=[
  {
    "question": "Can SvelteKit mix static pages with server-rendered routes?",
    "answer": "Yes. Page and layout options control prerendering, server rendering and client rendering. A service page can be generated at build time while a route that needs current or user-specific data runs on a server. We choose per route. Disabling server rendering across the entire site to accommodate one component is the mistake we look for first."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can we use SvelteKit form actions on static hosting?",
    "answer": "Not in a fully static export. Form actions need a server to handle the POST request, and a page with actions cannot be prerendered. A static page needs a separately supported form endpoint instead. Form handling and hosting therefore have to be agreed together, before either is built."
  },
  {
    "question": "What does progressive enhancement mean for our form?",
    "answer": "SvelteKit form actions can work through a normal HTML submission without client-side JavaScript. Enhancement then adds in-page feedback and pending states. We agree checks for validation, failed requests and confirmation in both paths, including where the enquiry is actually received."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do the adapter and host affect the build?",
    "answer": "The adapter creates output for the deployment target. A static adapter suits a fully prerendered site; server features need a compatible runtime and adapter. We confirm the host, environment settings and deployment process before relying on server routes or actions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will editors get a CMS with SvelteKit?",
    "answer": "No. SvelteKit is not an editor interface. A CMS integration, preview access and the route from an approved edit to a published page must be scoped. Build-time content normally needs a rebuild; server-loaded content also depends on the chosen caching behaviour. WordPress may be simpler if familiar publishing tools are the main requirement."
  },
  {
    "question": "What belongs in the handover?",
    "answer": "The agreed handover should identify the SvelteKit version, adapter, hosting accounts, CMS dependencies, build commands and rollback steps. Name who owns updates and form delivery after launch. We can work with your developer on SEO direction and QA. The framework choice does not guarantee rankings."
  }
];
---
<MainLayout title="SvelteKit Development Philippines | Bright Forge" description="SvelteKit development from the Philippines: server-rendered routes, prerendered content, forms and hosting planned together for SEO and easy maintenance." canonical={canonical} type="service" breadcrumbs={[{name:"Home",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/"},{name:"Development",url:"https://brightforge.com.ph/development/"},{name:"SvelteKit",url:canonical}]} faqs={faqs}>
<LegacyHeader slot="header" />
<Hero title="SvelteKit <span>Development</span>" breadcrumbCurrent="SvelteKit">
<Fragment slot="story"><p class="hero-subtitle">SvelteKit development for sites that combine published content with server-backed interactions.</p><p>SvelteKit renders pages on the server by default and lets each route opt into prerendering, which suits search. Its components compile to small scripts, which suits speed. The trade-off is a smaller ecosystem than React has. We plan which pages can be built ahead of time, which need live data and how forms reach the business. The CMS and hosting choices need to support that plan, beyond a successful local demo.</p><div class="hero-proof">{proof.map((item)=>(<article><strong>{item.title}</strong><span>{item.text}</span></article>))}</div></Fragment>
<Fragment slot="actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="#checks" text="See What We Check" /></Fragment>
</Hero>
<main class="dev-page">
<section id="checks"><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Build focus</p><h2>SvelteKit for SEO: server-rendered by default, with <span>routes, forms and deployment</span> planned together</h2><p>A content page can be prerendered, while a request form may need a server action. That difference affects hosting, publishing and failure handling. We map the routes and their dependencies before choosing an adapter.</p><p>For search, SvelteKit starts in a good place: a route returns real HTML unless someone switches server rendering off. The faults we look for are the ones introduced during the build. Server rendering is disabled site-wide to silence one browser-only component. A prerendered page still expects a form action that only a server can run. The adapter does not match the host, so routes that worked locally fail in production.</p><p>Illustrative build decision: prerender a service guide, then send its enquiry to a server-backed form route. Test a normal submission with JavaScript disabled and the enhanced interaction with it enabled. In both cases, confirm validation feedback and receipt by the agreed destination. This example is not a published client project.</p><p>We have not published a SvelteKit client project. The builds of ours you can inspect are this site, which runs on Astro, and <a href="/development/#workbench">Shinobi</a>, our own brand site for creative businesses, a React build on TanStack Start. A SvelteKit build is held to the same route, metadata and launch checks.</p></div><aside class="panel"><h3>What we check first</h3><p>Which routes need request-time data, where forms submit, how CMS edits become public and whether the proposed host runs the required adapter. Existing URLs, titles, canonicals and redirect rules remain part of launch planning.</p></aside></div></section>
<DevelopmentStackJobs
  title="Four decisions a <span>SvelteKit</span> route map has to record"
  intro="The route map should explain what runs at build time, in the browser and on the server."
  jobs={[
    {title:"Route rendering", text:"Prerender stable content where appropriate. Keep routes that require current or private data on a suitable server path, with public metadata checked separately."},
    {title:"Form actions", text:"Agree server-side validation, confirmation and the receiving system. Progressive enhancement should improve the submission experience. It must never conceal a failed request."},
    {title:"Adapter and deployment", text:"Match the output to the host. Check production environment settings, direct route visits and error responses as well as the development server."},
    {title:"Content changes", text:"Trace a CMS edit through preview, approval and publication. Record rebuild or cache requirements and who can roll back a release."}
  ]}
/>
<section><div class="page-container two-column"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who this suits</p><h2>Where <span>SvelteKit fits</span>, and what it asks of the team that maintains it</h2><p>SvelteKit suits fast marketing sites, interactive content projects and lean applications where performance matters and the public pages must still rank. The maintenance question is about people as much as code. Fewer developers work in Svelte than in React, and there are fewer ready-made CMS integrations and component libraries to lean on. Decide who will own the site after handover before choosing it.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>When a different stack fits</h3><p>For mostly editorial pages with a few interactive controls, compare Astro before taking on an application framework. If familiar editing, roles and publishing are the main need, compare WordPress. SvelteKit suits a team prepared to maintain its application and CMS integration.</p></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><p class="eyebrow">Related development services</p><h2>Compare SvelteKit with <span>content-first and editor-first builds</span></h2><div class="link-grid"><a href="/development/astro/"><span>Astro development</span>Mostly editorial pages with a few interactive controls.</a><a href="/development/wordpress/"><span>WordPress development</span>Familiar editing, roles and publishing for a content team.</a><a href="/development/nextjs/"><span>Next.js development</span>The React alternative, with a larger ecosystem to draw on.</a></div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container faq-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p><h2><span>SvelteKit development</span> questions on rendering, forms and hosting</h2></div><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map((faq)=>(<details class="faq-item"><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>))}</div></div></section>
<section><div class="page-container"><div class="cta-card"><h2>Considering <span>SvelteKit</span>? Check the routes, the forms and the host first.</h2><p>Send your goals, the current site and the content your team needs to edit. We will review the SEO risk and the maintenance picture before recommending SvelteKit or a safer alternative.</p><div class="cta-actions"><ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Request a Site Review" /><ButtonSecondary href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/" text="Technical SEO" /></div></div></div></section>
</main><Footer slot="footer"/>
</MainLayout>

